# Line-ending policy.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS (learned 2026-08-10): the MiSTer repo had a
# .gitattributes and it was dropped during the Pocket import. Every shell
# script then checked out with CRLF on this Windows box, and WSL refused to
# run them — `./check_boot.sh` fails with "cannot execute: required file not
# found", which is bash's unhelpful way of saying the `#!/bin/bash\r` shebang
# names a nonexistent interpreter. The Verilator harness runs under WSL, so
# LF is not cosmetic here, it is what makes the verification loop work.
#
# Scope note: the MiSTer version of this file pinned only exact paths, to
# avoid renormalising a repo with a historical mix of endings. This fork was
# imported as a flat snapshot from a single tree, so there is no such history
# to protect and the patterns can be broad.

# Anything executed by a shell (WSL, MSYS, CI) must be LF.
*.sh        text eol=lf
*.bash      text eol=lf

# Python and TCL are read by interpreters that mostly tolerate CRLF, but
# keeping them LF avoids surprises in shebang'd scripts.
*.py        text eol=lf
*.tcl       text eol=lf

# RTL and constraints: LF keeps diffs against ../MacLC_MiSTer readable.
# NOTE *.vhd is TEXT here — rtl/tg68k/ carries the CPU core's original VHDL
# (TG68K_ALU.vhd etc.). The other meaning of .vhd, a Virtual Hard Disk image,
# only ever appears under the gitignored releases/, so it is never tracked and
# there is no conflict.
*.v         text eol=lf
*.sv        text eol=lf
*.vh        text eol=lf
*.vhd       text eol=lf
*.sdc       text eol=lf
*.qip       text eol=lf
*.srf       text eol=lf

# Docs and config.
*.md        text eol=lf
*.json      text eol=lf
Makefile    text eol=lf

# Quartus rewrites these with CRLF on every compile on a Windows build box;
# pinning them stops that showing up as a whole-file diff.
*.qsf       text eol=crlf
*.qpf       text eol=crlf

# ROM/firmware images that LOOK binary but are ASCII text: one hex byte per
# line, consumed by $readmemh (rtl/egret_rom.hex, rtl/egret/egret_rom.hex,
# rtl/egret/egret.pram, verilator/rtl/*.hex). They must be eol=lf, not
# `binary` — marking them binary makes git store whatever the working tree
# happens to hold, which on a Windows checkout is CRLF, and that is how they
# silently ended up CRLF from the import until 2026-08-10. $readmemh tolerated
# the trailing \r, so nothing broke loudly; the files just stopped matching
# upstream byte-for-byte.
*.hex       text eol=lf
*.pram      text eol=lf

# Genuine binaries — never touch.
*.rom       binary
*.bin       binary
*.rbf       binary
*.rbf_r     binary
*.png       binary
*.dsk       binary
*.img       binary
*.hda       binary
*.nvr       binary
*.stp       binary
